WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:13.750 --> 00:20.320
Transcript of Chair Yellen's
Press Conference September 21,

00:20.320 --> 00:22.220
2016 CHAIR YELLEN.

00:22.220 --> 00:22.930
Good afternoon.

00:22.930 --> 00:24.790
At our meeting that concluded
earlier today, my colleagues

00:24.790 --> 00:27.580
and I on the Federal Open Market
Committee discussed overall

00:27.580 --> 00:31.750
economic conditions and
decided to keep the target range

00:31.750 --> 00:37.020
for the federal funds rate
at 1/4 to 1/2 percent.

00:37.020 --> 00:41.170
We judged that the case for
an increase has strengthened

00:41.170 --> 00:44.750
but decided for the time being
to wait for further evidence

00:44.750 --> 00:48.810
of continued progress
toward our objectives.

00:48.810 --> 00:52.090
Our current policy should help
move the economy toward our

00:52.090 --> 00:57.470
statutory goals of maximum
employment and price stability.

00:57.470 --> 01:00.870
I'll have more to say
about our decision shortly,

01:00.870 --> 01:04.060
but first I will review
recent economic developments

01:04.060 --> 01:05.730
and the outlook.

01:05.730 --> 01:09.550
Economic growth, which was
subdued during the first half

01:09.550 --> 01:12.640
of the year, appears
to have picked up.

01:12.640 --> 01:14.510
Household spending continues

01:14.510 --> 01:17.630
to be the key source
of that growth.

01:17.630 --> 01:20.610
This spending has been
supported by solid increases

01:20.610 --> 01:24.840
in household income as well
as by relatively high levels

01:24.840 --> 01:28.050
of consumer sentiment
and wealth.

01:28.050 --> 01:31.430
Business investment,
however, remains soft,

01:31.430 --> 01:34.930
both in the energy
sector and more broadly.

01:34.930 --> 01:38.960
The energy industry has
been hard hit by the drop

01:38.960 --> 01:43.800
in oil prices since
mid-2014, and investment

01:43.800 --> 01:46.000
in that sector continued
to contract

01:46.000 --> 01:48.690
through the first
half of the year.

01:48.690 --> 01:52.590
However, drilling is now
showing signs of stabilizing.

01:52.590 --> 01:55.650
Overall, we expect that
the economy will expand

01:55.650 --> 01:59.010
at a moderate pace over
the next few years.

01:59.010 --> 02:04.280
Turning to employment, job gains
averaged about 180,000 per month

02:04.280 --> 02:09.250
over the past four months, about
the same solid pace recorded

02:09.250 --> 02:12.050
since the beginning of the year.

02:12.050 --> 02:15.430
In the longer run,
that's well above the pace

02:15.430 --> 02:18.610
that we estimate is
needed to provide work

02:18.610 --> 02:21.830
for new entrants
in the job market.

02:21.830 --> 02:24.200
But so far this year,
most measures

02:24.200 --> 02:28.530
of labor market slack
have shown little change.

02:28.530 --> 02:33.010
The unemployment rate in
August-- 4.9 percent--

02:33.010 --> 02:36.040
was the same as in January.

02:36.040 --> 02:37.100
And a broader measure

02:37.100 --> 02:40.090
of unemployment has
also flattened out--

02:40.090 --> 02:43.970
a measure that includes people
who want and are available

02:43.970 --> 02:48.570
to work but have not searched
recently as well as people

02:48.570 --> 02:53.250
who are working part time but
would rather work full time.

02:53.250 --> 02:56.220
The fact that unemployment
measures have been holding

02:56.220 --> 03:01.020
steady while the number of
jobs has grown solidly shows

03:01.020 --> 03:03.940
that more people,
presumably in response

03:03.940 --> 03:07.790
to better employment
opportunities and higher wages,

03:07.790 --> 03:12.050
have started actively
seeking and finding jobs.

03:12.050 --> 03:14.990
This is a very welcome
development,

03:14.990 --> 03:17.590
both for the individuals
involved

03:17.590 --> 03:19.750
and the nation as a whole.

03:19.750 --> 03:23.790
We continue to expect that
labor market conditions will

03:23.790 --> 03:27.520
strengthen somewhat
further over time.

03:27.520 --> 03:29.180
Ongoing economic growth

03:29.180 --> 03:33.160
and an improving job market
are key factors supporting our

03:33.160 --> 03:35.380
inflation outlook.

03:35.380 --> 03:38.470
Overall consumer
price inflation--

03:38.470 --> 03:40.270
as measured by the price index

03:40.270 --> 03:43.180
for personal consumption
expenditures--

03:43.180 --> 03:48.260
was less than 1 percent over
the 12 months ending in July,

03:48.260 --> 03:51.890
still short of our
2 percent objective.

03:51.890 --> 03:55.530
Much of this shortfall continues
to reflect earlier declines

03:55.530 --> 03:58.420
in energy and import prices.

03:58.420 --> 04:02.520
Core inflation, which excludes
energy and food prices that tend

04:02.520 --> 04:05.240
to be more volatile
than other prices,

04:05.240 --> 04:08.810
has been running
about 1-1/2 percent.

04:08.810 --> 04:13.110
As transitory influences
holding down inflation fade

04:13.110 --> 04:15.680
and as the job market
strengthens further,

04:15.680 --> 04:19.420
we continue to expect
inflation to rise to 2 percent

04:19.420 --> 04:22.400
over the next two
to three years.

04:22.400 --> 04:26.080
Our inflation outlook also rests
importantly on our judgment

04:26.080 --> 04:30.030
that longer-run inflation
expectations remain reasonably

04:30.030 --> 04:31.630
well anchored.

04:31.630 --> 04:34.070
However, we can't
take the stability

04:34.070 --> 04:37.100
of longer-run inflation
expectations for granted,

04:37.100 --> 04:40.610
and we will continue to
carefully monitor actual

04:40.610 --> 04:44.610
and expected progress
toward our inflation goal.

04:44.610 --> 04:46.600
Indeed, we are fully committed

04:46.600 --> 04:50.670
to achieving our 2 percent
inflation objective.

04:50.670 --> 04:53.280
Let me turn to the
economic projections--

04:53.280 --> 04:56.100
now extending through 2019--

04:56.100 --> 04:57.790
that were submitted
for this meeting

04:57.790 --> 05:01.500
by the Federal Open Market
Committee participants.

05:01.500 --> 05:05.010
As always, participants
conditioned their projections

05:05.010 --> 05:08.600
on their own view of
appropriate monetary policy,

05:08.600 --> 05:12.340
which in turn depends on
each participant's assessment

05:12.340 --> 05:16.070
of the multitude of factors
that shape the outlook.

05:16.070 --> 05:17.870
The median projection for growth

05:17.870 --> 05:22.020
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product, or GDP,

05:22.020 --> 05:25.980
is 1.8 percent this year.

05:25.980 --> 05:29.210
This figure is somewhat
lower than projected in June

05:29.210 --> 05:32.590
as a result of the
weaker-than-expected growth seen

05:32.590 --> 05:34.150
in the first half of the year.

05:34.150 --> 05:40.500
In 2017 and 2018, the median
growth projection is unchanged

05:40.500 --> 05:44.520
at 2 percent, somewhat higher
than the median estimate

05:44.520 --> 05:46.370
of longer-run normal growth.

05:46.370 --> 05:52.100
In 2019, growth edges
down to 1.8 percent,

05:52.100 --> 05:56.170
in line with its estimated
longer-run rate, which has als--

05:56.170 --> 06:00.510
which has been revised
down a bit since June.

06:00.510 --> 06:04.630
The median projection for
the unemployment rate stands

06:04.630 --> 06:08.010
at 4.8 percent at
the end of this year,

06:08.010 --> 06:10.680
a touch higher than in June.

06:10.680 --> 06:12.620
Over the next three years,

06:12.620 --> 06:16.950
the median unemployment rate
runs near 4-1/2 percent,

06:16.950 --> 06:19.250
modestly below the
median estimate

06:19.250 --> 06:21.970
of its longer-run normal rate.

06:21.970 --> 06:27.500
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.3 percent this

06:27.500 --> 06:31.210
year and rises to
1.9 percent next year

06:31.210 --> 06:35.490
and 2 percent in 2018 and 2019.

06:35.490 --> 06:38.660
Returning to monetary
policy, the recent pickup

06:38.660 --> 06:41.500
in economic growth
and continued progress

06:41.500 --> 06:44.220
in the labor market have
strengthened the case

06:44.220 --> 06:47.710
for an increase in the
federal funds rate.

06:47.710 --> 06:50.670
Moreover, the Committee judges
the risks to the outlook

06:50.670 --> 06:53.080
to be roughly balanced.

06:53.080 --> 06:55.880
So why didn't we raise
the federal funds rate

06:55.880 --> 06:58.230
at today's meeting?

06:58.230 --> 07:00.870
Our decision does
not reflect a lack

07:00.870 --> 07:03.330
of confidence in the economy.

07:03.330 --> 07:06.800
Conditions in the labor
market are strengthening,

07:06.800 --> 07:09.620
and we expect that to continue.

07:09.620 --> 07:13.210
And while inflation remains
low, we expect it to rise

07:13.210 --> 07:16.590
to our 2 percent
objective over time.

07:16.590 --> 07:18.980
But with labor market
slack being taken

07:18.980 --> 07:23.090
up at a somewhat slower
pace than in previous years,

07:23.090 --> 07:25.120
scope for some further
improvement

07:25.120 --> 07:29.290
in the labor market remaining,
and inflation continuing

07:29.290 --> 07:33.390
to run below our 2 percent
target, we chose to wait

07:33.390 --> 07:34.910
for further evidence

07:34.910 --> 07:38.510
of continued progress
toward our objectives.

07:38.510 --> 07:43.220
This cautious approach to paring
back monetary policy support is

07:43.220 --> 07:45.290
all the more appropriate given

07:45.290 --> 07:48.560
that short-term interest
rates are still near zero,

07:48.560 --> 07:51.100
which means that we can
more effectively respond

07:51.100 --> 07:55.610
to surprisingly strong inflation
pressures in the future

07:55.610 --> 07:58.830
by raising rates than to
a weakening labor market

07:58.830 --> 08:01.670
and falling inflation
by cutting rates.

08:01.670 --> 08:04.940
We continue to expect
that the evolution

08:04.940 --> 08:08.760
of the economy will warrant
only gradual increases

08:08.760 --> 08:12.160
in the federal funds
rate over time to achieve

08:12.160 --> 08:14.470
and maintain our objectives.

08:14.470 --> 08:15.890
That's based on our view

08:15.890 --> 08:19.320
that the neutral nominal
federal funds rate--

08:19.320 --> 08:20.760
that is, the interest rate

08:20.760 --> 08:24.290
that is neither expansionary
nor contractionary

08:24.290 --> 08:28.430
and keeps the economy
operating on an even keel--

08:28.430 --> 08:32.570
is currently quite low
by historical standards.

08:32.570 --> 08:36.540
With the federal funds rate
modestly below the neutral rate,

08:36.540 --> 08:40.130
the current stance of monetary
policy should be viewed

08:40.130 --> 08:44.000
as modestly accommodative,
which is appropriate

08:44.000 --> 08:47.820
to foster further progress
toward our objectives.

08:47.820 --> 08:52.060
But since monetary policy is
only modestly accommodative,

08:52.060 --> 08:55.120
there appears little risk
of falling behind the curve

08:55.120 --> 08:58.450
in the near future,
and gradual increases

08:58.450 --> 09:02.550
in the federal funds rate will
likely be sufficient to get

09:02.550 --> 09:07.150
to a neutral policy stance
over the next few years.

09:07.150 --> 09:10.610
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

09:10.610 --> 09:13.420
of appropriate monetary policy.

09:13.420 --> 09:17.250
The median projection for the
federal funds rate rises only

09:17.250 --> 09:22.160
gradually to 1.1 percent
at the end of next year,

09:22.160 --> 09:25.670
1.9 percent at the end of 2018,

09:25.670 --> 09:29.900
and 2.6 percent by
the end of 2019.

09:29.900 --> 09:33.800
Compared with the projections
made in June, the median path

09:33.800 --> 09:38.040
for the federal funds rate
has been revised down 1/4

09:38.040 --> 09:40.770
to 1/2 percentage point.

09:40.770 --> 09:43.650
Most participants also
marked down their estimate

09:43.650 --> 09:47.080
of the longer-run normal
federal funds rate,

09:47.080 --> 09:50.630
with the median now
at 2.9 percent.

09:50.630 --> 09:53.520
As I have noted on
previous occasions,

09:53.520 --> 09:56.670
participants' projections
for the federal funds rate,

09:56.670 --> 09:58.650
including the median path,

09:58.650 --> 10:02.570
are not a fixed plan
for future policy.

10:02.570 --> 10:05.500
Policy is not on
a preset course.

10:05.500 --> 10:08.770
These forecasts represent
participants' individual

10:08.770 --> 10:13.120
assessments of appropriate
policy, given their projections

10:13.120 --> 10:17.920
of economic growth, employment,
inflation, and other factors

10:17.920 --> 10:20.730
at a particular point in time.

10:20.730 --> 10:23.990
However, the economic outlook
is inherently uncertain,

10:23.990 --> 10:27.070
and any assessment of
the appropriate path

10:27.070 --> 10:31.400
for the federal funds rate will
change in response to changes

10:31.400 --> 10:35.140
to the economic outlook
and associated risks.

10:35.140 --> 10:38.850
Finally, we will continue
to reinvest proceeds

10:38.850 --> 10:41.400
from maturing Treasury
securities

10:41.400 --> 10:43.780
and principal payments
from agency debt

10:43.780 --> 10:46.460
and mortgage-backed securities.

10:46.460 --> 10:48.260
As our statement says,

10:48.260 --> 10:52.500
we anticipate continuing this
policy "until normalization

10:52.500 --> 10:56.820
of the level of the federal
funds rate is well under way."

10:56.820 --> 10:58.840
Maintaining our sizable holdings

10:58.840 --> 11:02.660
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

11:02.660 --> 11:06.280
financial conditions and
should reduce the risk

11:06.280 --> 11:09.970
that we might have to lower
the federal funds rate to zero

11:09.970 --> 11:14.380
in the event of a future
large adverse shock.

11:14.380 --> 11:15.510
Thank you.

11:15.510 --> 11:18.420
I'd be happy to take
your questions.

11:18.420 --> 11:20.360
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:23.630 --> 11:25.590
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

11:25.590 --> 11:28.650
Madam Chair, critics of the
Federal Reserve have said

11:28.650 --> 11:32.240
that you look for any
excuse not to hike,

11:32.240 --> 11:34.640
that the goalposts
constantly move.

11:34.640 --> 11:37.350
And it looks, indeed, like
there are new goalposts now

11:37.350 --> 11:40.000
when you say looking for
"further evidence" and--

11:40.000 --> 11:44.080
and you suggest that it's
evidence that labor--

11:44.080 --> 11:46.880
labor market slack
is "being taken up."

11:46.880 --> 11:50.340
Could you explain what
"for the time being" means,

11:50.340 --> 11:52.120
in terms of a time frame,

11:52.120 --> 11:55.560
and what that further evidence
you would look for in order

11:55.560 --> 11:56.730
to hike interest rates?

11:56.730 --> 11:59.470
And also, this notion that
the goalposts seem to move,

11:59.470 --> 12:02.350
and that you've indeed
introduced a new goalpost

12:02.350 --> 12:03.020
with this statement.

12:03.020 --> 12:03.480
Thank you.

12:03.480 --> 12:04.080
CHAIR YELLEN.

12:04.080 --> 12:06.810
I'll try to respond
to those questions.

12:06.810 --> 12:12.330
Let me try to set out again how
the Committee sees the economy

12:12.330 --> 12:14.550
and what we're looking for.

12:14.550 --> 12:18.850
We're generally pleased with
how the U.S. economy is doing.

12:18.850 --> 12:21.350
Growth was weak in the
first half of the year;

12:21.350 --> 12:23.850
we're seeing definite evidence

12:23.850 --> 12:27.910
that the economy is now
expanding more strongly.

12:27.910 --> 12:31.910
As I mentioned, payroll gains in
recent months have been solid,

12:31.910 --> 12:36.420
averaging around 180,000
per month, which is less

12:36.420 --> 12:39.800
than the pace in 2015,
but, as I mentioned,

12:39.800 --> 12:43.760
it's well above what's needed
to provide jobs for new entrants

12:43.760 --> 12:46.650
into the labor force over time.

12:46.650 --> 12:49.390
The unemployment
rate is pretty close

12:49.390 --> 12:52.430
to most FOMC participants'
estimates

12:52.430 --> 12:55.570
of its longer-run
equilibrium value.

12:55.570 --> 12:59.110
But, as I mentioned, that
rate and other measures

12:59.110 --> 13:02.550
of labor utilization
are little changed

13:02.550 --> 13:05.140
since the beginning of the year.

13:05.140 --> 13:09.490
I don't see that as bad
news, because it may reflect

13:09.490 --> 13:12.940
that the strong labor
market is attracting people

13:12.940 --> 13:17.140
from outside the labor
force back into employment.

13:17.140 --> 13:19.800
The labor force participation
rates increased

13:19.800 --> 13:21.640
on balance since late last year.

13:21.640 --> 13:28.060
It has-- it is on a declining
demographic trend, and the fact

13:28.060 --> 13:31.820
that it's increased shows
a substantial number

13:31.820 --> 13:35.080
of people are being attracted
into the labor market.

13:35.080 --> 13:38.950
The employment-to-population
ratio has also continued

13:38.950 --> 13:39.770
to increase.

13:39.770 --> 13:43.290
Now, we were not really
certain that this is something

13:43.290 --> 13:46.470
that would happen as the
labor market strengthened,

13:46.470 --> 13:51.560
and it's good to see that
development has taken place.

13:51.560 --> 13:56.840
And that is some news that
we've received in recent months,

13:56.840 --> 13:59.790
that the labor market
does have that potential

13:59.790 --> 14:01.360
to have people come back

14:01.360 --> 14:05.200
in without the unemployment
rate coming down.

14:05.200 --> 14:07.710
So we're not seeing
strong pressures

14:07.710 --> 14:11.230
on utilization suggesting
overheating,

14:11.230 --> 14:14.850
and my assessment would
be, based on this evidence,

14:14.850 --> 14:18.370
that the economy has a
little more room to run

14:18.370 --> 14:21.080
than might have been
previously thought.

14:21.080 --> 14:22.710
That's good news.

14:22.710 --> 14:26.460
Remember that inflation
continues below 2 percent,

14:26.460 --> 14:31.270
although we expect it
to move up over time.

14:31.270 --> 14:35.650
So the Committee
agrees that risks

14:35.650 --> 14:38.230
to the outlook have
become roughly balanced.

14:38.230 --> 14:40.830
We expect labor market
conditions

14:40.830 --> 14:43.190
to continue strengthening.

14:43.190 --> 14:48.340
And we are generally agreed
that gradual increases

14:48.340 --> 14:52.650
in the federal funds rate to
remove what is a modest degree

14:52.650 --> 14:56.620
of accommodation
will be appropriate.

14:56.620 --> 15:02.050
But we don't see the
economy as overheating now.

15:02.050 --> 15:07.150
My colleagues and I exchanged
views at this meeting

15:07.150 --> 15:09.920
on the appropriate
timing of the next step

15:09.920 --> 15:14.020
in reducing policy stimulus.

15:14.020 --> 15:17.460
Most of us judged that the
case for an immediate increase

15:17.460 --> 15:20.570
in the federal funds
rate is stronger,

15:20.570 --> 15:25.660
but that it would be
sensible, given the finding

15:25.660 --> 15:27.570
of a bit more running room,

15:27.570 --> 15:30.650
to wait to see some
continued progress--

15:30.650 --> 15:34.500
evidence that we continue to
progress toward our objectives.

15:34.500 --> 15:36.620
So, for the time being,

15:36.620 --> 15:40.210
we're going to watch incoming
evidence, and you can see

15:40.210 --> 15:44.890
from the SEP that most
participants do expect

15:44.890 --> 15:49.410
that one increase in the federal
funds rate will be appropriate

15:49.410 --> 15:50.490
this year.

15:50.490 --> 15:54.830
And I would expect to
see that if we continue

15:54.830 --> 15:57.910
on the current course of
labor market improvement

15:57.910 --> 16:02.840
and there are no major new risks
that develop and we simply stay

16:02.840 --> 16:04.220
on the current course.

16:04.220 --> 16:05.940
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

16:05.940 --> 16:07.790
Hi. Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

16:07.790 --> 16:08.810
Chair, thanks for this.

16:08.810 --> 16:11.150
I was wondering if you
could comment a little bit

16:11.150 --> 16:14.890
on the apparent tension
between the steady drift

16:14.890 --> 16:19.720
down in the long-run rate and
the steady drift down in some

16:19.720 --> 16:23.460
of the projections and
the seeming march toward a

16:23.460 --> 16:24.040
rate hike.

16:24.040 --> 16:26.900
If the neutral rate's
coming down over time

16:26.900 --> 16:29.370
and continues coming down and
you're eating up accommodation

16:29.370 --> 16:32.310
that way anyway, why not
wait for the dust to settle

16:32.310 --> 16:34.340
on that before moving rates up?

16:34.340 --> 16:34.720
CHAIR YELLEN.

16:34.720 --> 16:36.660
So it is true that our estimates

16:36.660 --> 16:39.810
of the neutral rate
are coming down,

16:39.810 --> 16:44.090
and that's what's largely
responsible for that shift.

16:44.090 --> 16:50.360
At the same time, we
generally agree that the stance

16:50.360 --> 16:54.060
of monetary policy is
somewhat accommodative.

16:54.060 --> 17:00.720
So 180,000 jobs a month is a
faster pace of employment growth

17:00.720 --> 17:03.740
than is sustainable
in the longer run.

17:03.740 --> 17:06.820
Now, we have seen people
come into the labor force

17:06.820 --> 17:08.760
and maybe more than
would be expected,

17:08.760 --> 17:12.340
which is why the
unemployment rate hasn't fallen.

17:12.340 --> 17:16.240
But that's probably not
something that is possible

17:16.240 --> 17:21.580
without the economy overheating
on an indefinite basis.

17:21.580 --> 17:25.290
So policy needs to
be forward looking.

17:25.290 --> 17:27.890
We don't want the
economy to overheat

17:27.890 --> 17:32.720
and significantly overshoot our
2 percent inflation objective.

17:32.720 --> 17:35.990
That's one risk that
we need to address.

17:35.990 --> 17:41.340
And I think we generally agree
that some gradual increases

17:41.340 --> 17:44.810
to remove that accommodation
will be appropriate

17:44.810 --> 17:46.760
if we stay on this course.

17:46.760 --> 17:50.730
But, as I emphasized, it's
not that much accommodation,

17:50.730 --> 17:53.720
and the economy has
shown evidence

17:53.720 --> 17:55.710
that there are more people

17:55.710 --> 17:59.650
who are being attracted
back into the labor force.

17:59.650 --> 18:03.650
So, in that sense, I
would characterize it as,

18:03.650 --> 18:06.690
we found the economy has
a bit more running room.

18:06.690 --> 18:10.610
Nevertheless, we don't want
the economy to overheat,

18:10.610 --> 18:14.930
and if things continue
on the current course,

18:14.930 --> 18:18.950
I think that some gradual
increases will be appropriate.

18:18.950 --> 18:23.640
And, mainly, what we discussed
today were issues affecting the

18:23.640 --> 18:26.490
timing of such increases.

18:26.490 --> 18:28.480
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

18:31.030 --> 18:33.160
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

18:33.160 --> 18:35.940
Last month in your
speech at Jackson Hole,

18:35.940 --> 18:37.630
you seemed to raise expectations

18:37.630 --> 18:40.390
that there could be a
rate hike in September.

18:40.390 --> 18:43.770
Other Fed officials talked,
including Vice Chairman Fischer.

18:43.770 --> 18:46.920
They seemed to support that.

18:46.920 --> 18:50.780
Fed President Rosengren
had some comments

18:50.780 --> 18:52.860
that sent the markets plunging.

18:52.860 --> 18:57.440
Then we had-- Governor
Brainard seemed to draw back.

18:57.440 --> 19:00.310
Is this hurting the Fed's
credibility do you think,

19:00.310 --> 19:03.070
or is this just a normal thing
that we should be looking

19:03.070 --> 19:06.970
for at this time-- uncertain
time with the economy?

19:06.970 --> 19:07.240
CHAIR YELLEN.

19:07.240 --> 19:11.020
Well, I did say at Jackson
Hole that I thought the case

19:11.020 --> 19:13.890
for a rate increase
had strengthened,

19:13.890 --> 19:19.770
and that assessment is
included in today's statement.

19:19.770 --> 19:22.910
So I think most of
my colleagues agree--

19:22.910 --> 19:25.330
agree with that assessment.

19:25.330 --> 19:27.610
I think we are trying

19:27.610 --> 19:30.590
to understand some
difficult issues.

19:30.590 --> 19:37.720
There is less disagreement among
participants in the Committee

19:37.720 --> 19:43.120
than you might think, listening
to speeches and commentary.

19:43.120 --> 19:47.220
I think we all agree that the
economy is making progress,

19:47.220 --> 19:50.360
that we are close to
an unemployment rate

19:50.360 --> 19:55.270
that is one that's
sustainable in the longer run.

19:55.270 --> 19:59.110
We all agree we are
undershooting our inflation

19:59.110 --> 20:02.270
goal, and that we want to
make sure we stay on a course

20:02.270 --> 20:05.240
that raises that to 2 percent.

20:05.240 --> 20:09.100
And we're struggling with
a difficult set of issues

20:09.100 --> 20:12.430
about what is the "new
normal" in this economy

20:12.430 --> 20:15.780
and in the global
economy more generally,

20:15.780 --> 20:21.460
which explains why we keep
revising down the rate path.

20:21.460 --> 20:27.190
And, you know, it's very
important that in a body

20:27.190 --> 20:32.050
like ours that a whole range
of views are expressed,

20:32.050 --> 20:36.650
that we have independent-minded
people who gather together

20:36.650 --> 20:40.700
and discuss these issues.

20:40.700 --> 20:45.610
My colleagues do explain, in
their individual speeches,

20:45.610 --> 20:47.690
their own perspectives.

20:47.690 --> 20:50.740
These are complicated,
complex issues,

20:50.740 --> 20:54.570
and it just isn't
straightforward exactly how

20:54.570 --> 20:57.540
to interpret what is
appropriate policy

20:57.540 --> 21:01.810
and exactly what is
going on in the economy.

21:01.810 --> 21:06.450
My-- my sense is that
market participants

21:06.450 --> 21:10.440
and the public more generally
learn more about the issues

21:10.440 --> 21:13.290
that we're grappling
with as they listen

21:13.290 --> 21:16.080
to this set of speeches.

21:16.080 --> 21:19.190
And I think it's
a very good thing

21:19.190 --> 21:23.440
that the FOMC is not a body
that suffers from groupthink.

21:23.440 --> 21:28.050
And you see that-- you see
that's one of the, you know,

21:28.050 --> 21:30.170
real worries in an organization,

21:30.170 --> 21:32.390
that everybody thinks
identically.

21:32.390 --> 21:35.730
But there's a lot that we
share in common and express,

21:35.730 --> 21:38.110
both in our statement
and in our speeches.

21:38.110 --> 21:40.110
And we are debating

21:40.110 --> 21:44.270
and discussing issues
pertaining to timing.

21:45.710 --> 21:47.650
JON HILSENRATH.

21:47.650 --> 21:49.950
Jon Hilsenrath from the
Wall Street Journal.

21:49.950 --> 21:53.610
Chair Yellen, Donald Trump, the
Republican presidential nominee,

21:53.610 --> 21:56.550
has charged that the Fed
is keeping interest rates

21:56.550 --> 22:00.040
artificially low to support
the Obama Administration.

22:00.040 --> 22:03.000
I'd like to hear what you
have to say to that charge.

22:03.000 --> 22:05.020
And, on a related note,
I wanted to ask you

22:05.020 --> 22:06.960
about the Fed's next
policy meeting,

22:06.960 --> 22:10.520
which is in early November, a
week before the next election--

22:10.520 --> 22:13.910
given that the case for
raising rates, you say today,

22:13.910 --> 22:17.430
has strengthened, should the
public see that November meeting

22:17.430 --> 22:20.590
as a live meeting when a
rate action could happen?

22:20.590 --> 22:21.300
Thank you.

22:21.300 --> 22:21.760
CHAIR YELLEN.

22:21.760 --> 22:26.060
Well, I think Congress very
wisely established the Federal

22:26.060 --> 22:29.970
Reserve as an independent
agency in order

22:29.970 --> 22:34.900
to insulate monetary policy from
short-term political pressures.

22:34.900 --> 22:40.770
And I can say emphatically that
partisan politics plays no role

22:40.770 --> 22:44.140
in our decisions about
the appropriate stance

22:44.140 --> 22:46.140
of monetary policy.

22:46.140 --> 22:51.640
We are trying to decide
what the best policy is

22:51.640 --> 22:55.580
to foster price stability
and maximum employment

22:55.580 --> 23:00.640
and to manage the variety
of risks that we see

23:00.640 --> 23:02.660
as affecting the outlook.

23:02.660 --> 23:05.370
We do not discuss
politics at our meetings,

23:05.370 --> 23:10.160
and we do not take politics
into account in our decisions.

23:10.160 --> 23:13.650
As I said, we're
generally pleased

23:13.650 --> 23:16.200
with the progress
of the economy.

23:16.200 --> 23:20.790
And the decision not to
raise rates today and to wait

23:20.790 --> 23:24.180
for some further evidence
that we're continuing

23:24.180 --> 23:28.210
on this course is largely
based on the judgment

23:28.210 --> 23:32.340
that we're not seeing evidence
that the economy is overheating,

23:32.340 --> 23:38.790
and that we are seeing evidence
that people are being drawn in--

23:38.790 --> 23:42.720
in larger numbers than at
least I would have expected--

23:42.720 --> 23:46.470
into the labor market, and that
that's healthy to continue,

23:46.470 --> 23:50.430
but that, nevertheless, we do
need to be forward looking.

23:50.430 --> 23:52.860
And if we continue
along this course,

23:52.860 --> 23:58.010
it likely will be appropriate
to raise the federal funds rate.

23:58.010 --> 24:00.300
And November you
asked about as well.

24:00.300 --> 24:04.720
Well, every meeting is live,
and we will again assess,

24:04.720 --> 24:09.240
as we always do, incoming
evidence in November

24:09.240 --> 24:14.770
and decide whether or
not a move is warranted.

24:14.770 --> 24:16.510
CRAIG TORRES.

24:16.510 --> 24:19.590
Madam Chair, Craig
Torres from Bloomberg.

24:19.590 --> 24:23.130
What observable data would
convince you and the Committee

24:23.130 --> 24:27.160
that this neutral federal funds
rate is starting to move up?

24:27.160 --> 24:29.200
There's a popular piece
of research by one

24:29.200 --> 24:32.650
of your colleagues that suggests
that it's at zero right now.

24:32.650 --> 24:34.870
And, second, I'm struck
by your opening remarks

24:34.870 --> 24:37.750
that the economy is--
isn't overheating.

24:37.750 --> 24:41.130
But does-- does that mean the
Committee sees this global

24:41.130 --> 24:43.420
reach-for-yield going
on right now

24:43.420 --> 24:45.730
as very low cost to its policy?

24:45.730 --> 24:48.290
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

24:48.290 --> 24:51.570
So you asked, first: What
evidence would suggest

24:51.570 --> 24:54.720
that the neutral real
rate is moving up?

24:54.720 --> 24:57.110
Well, I think if
you saw us revising

24:57.110 --> 25:02.640
up our growth forecasts,
revising down our estimates,

25:02.640 --> 25:05.920
well, with an unchanged
path for policy, you know,

25:05.920 --> 25:08.440
if you saw this, you
would see revisions

25:08.440 --> 25:09.960
in the funds rate path.

25:09.960 --> 25:12.810
But if unemployment
were moving down faster

25:12.810 --> 25:18.280
than we had anticipated,
if we saw faster growth

25:18.280 --> 25:22.810
or upward pressure on inflation,
that would be suggestive

25:22.810 --> 25:26.800
of the appropriateness
of reevaluating whether

25:26.800 --> 25:31.100
or not the neutral funds
rate had increased.

25:31.100 --> 25:34.700
I mean, the downward revisions
reflect the fact that,

25:34.700 --> 25:38.940
while the economy has made a
lot of progress, it's only made

25:38.940 --> 25:43.400
that progress in the
context of a monetary policy

25:43.400 --> 25:47.360
that has been characterized by
extremely low interest rates

25:47.360 --> 25:54.350
and negative real yields for
a very long period of time.

25:54.350 --> 25:58.070
Let's see, and then you
asked about global factors.

25:58.070 --> 26:03.090
So global factors, capital
flows-- CRAIG TORRES.

26:03.090 --> 26:05.330
-- about the global
reach-for-yield

26:05.330 --> 26:07.880
and whether the Committee
saw that as a cost

26:07.880 --> 26:09.680
to its accommodative
policy right now.

26:09.680 --> 26:12.160
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:12.160 --> 26:16.180
So, in most advanced
nations now,

26:16.180 --> 26:19.910
we have highly accommodative
policies, and they seem

26:19.910 --> 26:24.480
to be necessary for
countries to be able

26:24.480 --> 26:28.660
to achieve their inflation
and employment objectives.

26:28.660 --> 26:31.550
And that's characteristic of a--

26:31.550 --> 26:36.410
an environment in which
the neutral rate--

26:36.410 --> 26:40.350
interest rates both here
and in advanced countries

26:40.350 --> 26:44.160
around the globe
appear to be very low.

26:44.160 --> 26:49.770
And that is an environment that,
if we do have to live with that

26:49.770 --> 26:53.610
for a long time, we have to be
aware that it does give rise

26:53.610 --> 26:58.280
to a reach for yield
as individuals

26:58.280 --> 27:04.370
and investors seek to,
perhaps, take on risk

27:04.370 --> 27:10.880
or lengthen maturities to seek
higher-- to seek higher yields.

27:10.880 --> 27:13.920
And I think we should
be concerned about that

27:13.920 --> 27:18.030
to the extent it creates
financial stability risks.

27:18.030 --> 27:22.470
And we are very aware
that those are possible.

27:22.470 --> 27:29.350
We engage in regular assessments
of financial stability factors

27:29.350 --> 27:32.190
that bear on financial
stability.

27:32.190 --> 27:36.690
Overall, I would
say that the threats

27:36.690 --> 27:39.520
to financial stability
I would characterize,

27:39.520 --> 27:41.820
at this point, as moderate.

27:41.820 --> 27:47.420
Not-- I mean-- so, I would
characterize it as moderate.

27:47.420 --> 27:52.130
In general, I would not say that
asset valuations are out of line

27:52.130 --> 27:57.060
with historical norms, but there
are areas my colleague President

27:57.060 --> 28:00.660
Rosengren has focused on:
commercial real estate,

28:00.660 --> 28:04.160
where price-to-rent
ratios are very high

28:04.160 --> 28:06.730
or cap rates are very low.

28:06.730 --> 28:11.860
And that's something that
has caught our attention.

28:11.860 --> 28:15.840
We have a variety of tools
other than monetary policy

28:15.840 --> 28:18.070
to address such risks.

28:18.070 --> 28:22.340
We've recently issued new
supervisory guidance pertaining

28:22.340 --> 28:24.250
to commercial real estate.

28:24.250 --> 28:27.450
I would say, in the area
of commercial real estate,

28:27.450 --> 28:31.030
while valuations are high,
we are seeing some tightening

28:31.030 --> 28:35.810
of lending standards and
less debt growth associated

28:35.810 --> 28:39.840
with that rise in
commercial real estate prices.

28:39.840 --> 28:42.750
But, more generally,
we're not seeing signs

28:42.750 --> 28:49.160
of leverage building up
or maturity transformation

28:49.160 --> 28:51.880
in the way that we saw in
the run-up to the crisis,

28:51.880 --> 28:55.130
and we're keeping
a close eye on it.

28:55.130 --> 28:56.380
SAM FLEMING.

28:56.380 --> 28:57.810
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

28:57.810 --> 28:59.810
Two quasi-related questions.

28:59.810 --> 29:03.200
One, Bill Dudley earlier
this year suggested

29:03.200 --> 29:06.150
that political uncertainty
in the U.S. may be one

29:06.150 --> 29:08.770
of the depressants on business
investment at the moment.

29:08.770 --> 29:11.530
I wondered if you'd seen
any further evidence

29:11.530 --> 29:13.080
that election risk
was one of the reasons

29:13.080 --> 29:15.760
that businesses are
holding back at the margin.

29:15.760 --> 29:18.840
A second was a follow-up
on your Jackson Hole speech

29:18.840 --> 29:21.940
where you presented a fairly
optimistic sense of the scope

29:21.940 --> 29:24.100
for further monetary stimulus.

29:24.100 --> 29:25.030
You did raise the question

29:25.030 --> 29:26.930
of automatic stabilizers
in the U.S., however.

29:26.930 --> 29:31.060
Are you concerned that there
is insufficient fiscal backup

29:31.060 --> 29:33.650
to the Fed, and too much
is effectively being lumped

29:33.650 --> 29:34.980
on the shoulders
of the central bank

29:34.980 --> 29:36.620
if there is a fresh downturn?

29:36.620 --> 29:37.890
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

29:37.890 --> 29:41.000
Well, starting with the issue
of political uncertainty

29:41.000 --> 29:45.410
and investment, investment
spending really has been quite

29:45.410 --> 29:50.710
weak for some time, and we're
really not certain exactly what

29:50.710 --> 29:52.240
is causing that.

29:52.240 --> 29:55.640
Part of it, of course, has
been the huge contraction

29:55.640 --> 30:00.460
in drilling activity associated
with falling oil prices,

30:00.460 --> 30:06.900
but the weakness in investment
spending extends beyond--

30:06.900 --> 30:09.400
beyond that sector,
and I'm not certain

30:09.400 --> 30:12.630
of exactly what explains
that, whether--

30:12.630 --> 30:15.260
I'm not aware of
evidence that suggests

30:15.260 --> 30:20.090
that it's political
uncertainty, but it certainly--

30:20.090 --> 30:23.070
I would agree with the
finding that it has been weak.

30:23.070 --> 30:25.630
Consumer sentiment
is perfectly solid.

30:25.630 --> 30:28.890
We're seeing a lot of
strength in consumer spending,

30:28.890 --> 30:35.600
and consumer sentiment
certainly seems to be solid.

30:35.600 --> 30:41.010
You asked about scope for
further monetary policy action.

30:41.010 --> 30:44.450
I was careful in Jackson Hole.

30:44.450 --> 30:47.270
I indicated we have
a number of tools

30:47.270 --> 30:51.690
that we've used before
and could use again.

30:51.690 --> 30:56.230
I did indicate that
I do have concerns

30:56.230 --> 30:58.350
about the scope for
monetary policy.

30:58.350 --> 31:02.610
Nevertheless, at this point
our balance sheet is large,

31:02.610 --> 31:07.170
and we're not at what we
see as the normal level--

31:07.170 --> 31:09.500
longer-run level
of interest rates.

31:09.500 --> 31:12.550
So, at the moment, the
funds rate is very low.

31:12.550 --> 31:14.570
It's below that normal level.

31:14.570 --> 31:18.550
So, at the moment, I would
say the zero lower bound is a

31:18.550 --> 31:23.400
concern, and we have less
scope than I would like to see

31:23.400 --> 31:27.600
or expect us to have
in the longer run.

31:27.600 --> 31:30.250
Now, I think it would be--

31:30.250 --> 31:34.100
it would be worthwhile for
other policymakers to think

31:34.100 --> 31:36.160
about what role they could play

31:36.160 --> 31:38.940
in addressing negative
shocks should they come.

31:38.940 --> 31:43.520
And I mentioned specifically
automatic stabilizers

31:43.520 --> 31:46.280
because I think that's
an important way

31:46.280 --> 31:51.430
in which fiscal policy serves to
cushion shocks to the economy.

31:51.430 --> 31:55.470
And it would seem to me,
without getting into specifics,

31:55.470 --> 32:00.170
that there are ways in which
the response of fiscal policy

32:00.170 --> 32:03.670
to shifts in the economy
could be strengthened,

32:03.670 --> 32:09.840
which would help take some
burden off monetary policy.

32:11.070 --> 32:13.250
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

32:13.250 --> 32:15.650
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

32:15.650 --> 32:17.820
In the run-up to the Brexit
vote earlier this year,

32:17.820 --> 32:20.420
several Fed policymakers
cited it as a reason

32:20.420 --> 32:22.710
that they were reluctant
to raise rates in June

32:22.710 --> 32:25.360
because of the uncertainty
associated with that vote.

32:25.360 --> 32:26.980
In the run-up to the
presidential election,

32:26.980 --> 32:28.780
I have not heard any
Fed policymaker give

32:28.780 --> 32:30.800
that as a reason
that they might want

32:30.800 --> 32:32.830
to delay raising
rates in November.

32:32.830 --> 32:35.200
Could you explain why
the Fed regards Brexit

32:35.200 --> 32:37.210
as a greater danger to
the American economy

32:37.210 --> 32:40.080
than the presidential election
that's actually happening here?

32:40.080 --> 32:43.030
And, second, there were three
dissents at this meeting.

32:43.030 --> 32:47.020
Could you explain what the
cause of disagreement was,

32:47.020 --> 32:48.820
what those policymakers thought?

32:48.820 --> 32:50.140
CHAIR YELLEN.

32:50.140 --> 32:53.360
So we are very focused
on evaluating,

32:53.360 --> 32:56.800
given the way the
economy is operating,

32:56.800 --> 33:00.730
what is the right policy
to foster our goals,

33:00.730 --> 33:03.420
and I'm not going to
get into politics.

33:03.420 --> 33:09.030
I'm just-- those are factors
that we don't consider,

33:09.030 --> 33:10.840
and I don't-- I'm not
going to get involved

33:10.840 --> 33:13.670
in commenting on the election.

33:13.670 --> 33:17.100
In terms of the dissents,
as I indicated,

33:17.100 --> 33:21.080
the notion that we do
have some accommodation,

33:21.080 --> 33:25.050
that if we continue
on the current path,

33:25.050 --> 33:31.740
it's something we will
need to remove over time.

33:31.740 --> 33:35.920
There's general agreement
among participants on that,

33:35.920 --> 33:39.470
but the precise timing
of what is the right--

33:39.470 --> 33:41.610
what is the right
timing for removing

33:41.610 --> 33:43.900
that accommodation is something

33:43.900 --> 33:47.180
on which we had active
discussions,

33:47.180 --> 33:49.890
and there are a range
of opinions.

33:49.890 --> 33:55.460
And the dissents represent a
judgment on the part of some

33:55.460 --> 33:58.200
of my colleagues
that it's important

33:58.200 --> 34:00.720
to begin that process now.

34:00.720 --> 34:05.870
I certainly agree, and I've said
myself, that there are risks

34:05.870 --> 34:09.900
in waiting too long to
remove accommodation,

34:09.900 --> 34:14.820
and we need to take a
forward-looking approach.

34:14.820 --> 34:20.000
I've always advocated making
policy based on forecasts

34:20.000 --> 34:26.160
of where the economy is heading
and taking account of risks.

34:26.160 --> 34:28.940
And there are two
particular risks that we need

34:28.940 --> 34:30.450
to think about and balance.

34:30.450 --> 34:36.910
One is the risk that the economy
runs too hot, that unemploy--

34:36.910 --> 34:40.480
the labor market tightens too
much, that unemployment falls

34:40.480 --> 34:45.560
to a very low level, that
we need to tighten policy

34:45.560 --> 34:49.570
in a less gradual way than would
be ideal, and in the course

34:49.570 --> 34:53.920
of doing that, because that
is a very difficult thing

34:53.920 --> 34:58.930
to accomplish, to gently
create a bit more slack

34:58.930 --> 35:01.860
in the labor market, we
could cause a recession

35:01.860 --> 35:03.240
in the process.

35:03.240 --> 35:05.720
And so that's something
my colleagues

35:05.720 --> 35:09.930
and I certainly wouldn't
want to be responsible for.

35:09.930 --> 35:13.880
We would all like to have
a very long expansion,

35:13.880 --> 35:18.590
with the labor market operating
well for many years to come,

35:18.590 --> 35:23.160
and the prospect that
we could create--

35:23.160 --> 35:29.120
create downside risk for the
labor market is something we

35:29.120 --> 35:32.020
would like to avoid,
and taking "a stitch

35:32.020 --> 35:35.430
in time" might be
essential to avoiding that.

35:35.430 --> 35:38.570
On the other hand, inflation
is running below our 2 percent

35:38.570 --> 35:40.780
objective, and it's
also important

35:40.780 --> 35:43.670
that we make sure we
get back to 2 percent.

35:43.670 --> 35:48.570
And I have routinely
indicated a number of measures

35:48.570 --> 35:52.010
of inflation expectations that
are running at the low ends

35:52.010 --> 35:55.620
of their historical range, and
we're watching that as well.

35:55.620 --> 35:57.430
And there would also be risks

35:57.430 --> 36:02.510
from not seeing inflation move
back to our 2 percent objective.

36:02.510 --> 36:06.860
And exactly how to
balance these two risks,

36:06.860 --> 36:11.110
which is more serious-- which
is a more serious risk--

36:11.110 --> 36:15.020
can affect one's judgment
about the appropriate timing,

36:15.020 --> 36:18.490
and we're all struggling
to understand the magnitude

36:18.490 --> 36:21.170
and nature of those two risks.

36:21.170 --> 36:23.730
REBECCA JARVIS.

36:23.730 --> 36:25.230
Rebecca Jarvis, ABC News.

36:25.230 --> 36:28.870
Chair Yellen, at a time when
the public is losing faith

36:28.870 --> 36:33.090
in many institutions, did the
FOMC discuss the importance

36:33.090 --> 36:37.040
of today as an opportunity
to dispel the thinking

36:37.040 --> 36:39.730
that the Fed is politically
compromised

36:39.730 --> 36:41.130
or beholden to markets?

36:41.130 --> 36:42.580
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:42.580 --> 36:46.480
The Federal Reserve is not
politically compromised.

36:46.480 --> 36:49.350
We do not discuss
politics in our meetings.

36:49.350 --> 36:52.830
I can't recall any meeting
that I have ever attended

36:52.830 --> 36:56.120
where politics has been
a matter of discussion.

36:56.120 --> 37:00.290
I think the public, if they
had been watching our meeting

37:00.290 --> 37:06.070
on TV today, would have felt
that we had a rich, deep,

37:06.070 --> 37:11.260
serious, intellectual
debate about the risks

37:11.260 --> 37:13.680
and the forecasts
for the economy,

37:13.680 --> 37:18.100
and we struggled
mightily with trying

37:18.100 --> 37:22.000
to understand one another's
points of view and to come

37:22.000 --> 37:26.220
out at a balanced place
and to act responsibly.

37:26.220 --> 37:29.370
And that's my commitment
to the American people,

37:29.370 --> 37:31.850
that I want to lead
an institution

37:31.850 --> 37:34.490
that is not political and is--

37:34.490 --> 37:38.050
that we are striving
to do our very best

37:38.050 --> 37:41.430
to pursue the goals the
Congress has assigned to us,

37:41.430 --> 37:43.960
which are important
ones of price stability

37:43.960 --> 37:45.020
and maximum employment.

37:45.020 --> 37:45.740
REBECCA JARVIS.

37:45.740 --> 37:49.000
Does it concern you, given
what Donald Trump has said

37:49.000 --> 37:53.210
at this point about the Federal
Reserve, that he could go back,

37:53.210 --> 37:56.250
if he were President, and
look at the minutes and look

37:56.250 --> 38:00.520
for signs of the Fed being
politically motivated

38:00.520 --> 38:01.530
and find them?

38:01.530 --> 38:02.140
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:02.140 --> 38:04.360
I have no concern that the pol--

38:04.360 --> 38:07.530
the Fed is politically
motivated, and I will assure

38:07.530 --> 38:11.810
that you will not find any
signs of political motivation

38:11.810 --> 38:15.310
when the transcripts are
released in five years.

38:15.310 --> 38:19.570
We-- I-- it is important that
we maintain the confidence

38:19.570 --> 38:23.630
of the public, and I do
believe that we deserve it.

38:23.630 --> 38:26.260
I know that these are
difficult decisions,

38:26.260 --> 38:29.930
and everybody may
not agree with them,

38:29.930 --> 38:33.660
but I hope the public will
understand that we're striving

38:33.660 --> 38:35.980
to do our best to
pursue these goals

38:35.980 --> 38:38.200
that do matter to all of us.

38:38.200 --> 38:39.850
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

38:39.850 --> 38:41.730
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

38:41.730 --> 38:43.990
You mentioned commercial
real estate.

38:43.990 --> 38:47.120
Are you worried that bubbles
could form in the economy

38:47.120 --> 38:50.510
because of our prolonged
low interest rates?

38:50.510 --> 38:51.050
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:51.050 --> 38:54.210
Yes. Of course, we-- we are
worried that bubbles could form

38:54.210 --> 39:00.910
in the economy, and we routinely
monitor asset valuations.

39:00.910 --> 39:04.450
While nobody can know
for sure what type

39:04.450 --> 39:07.530
of valuation represents
a bubble--

39:07.530 --> 39:11.920
that's only something one
can tell in hindsight--

39:11.920 --> 39:16.520
we are monitoring these
measures of valuation,

39:16.520 --> 39:21.270
and commercial real
estate valuations are high.

39:21.270 --> 39:25.450
Rents have moved up
over time, but, still,

39:25.450 --> 39:28.170
valuations are high
relative to rents.

39:28.170 --> 39:30.500
And so it is something
we've discussed.

39:30.500 --> 39:34.350
We called this out in our
Monetary Policy Report

39:34.350 --> 39:36.340
and in other presentations.

39:36.340 --> 39:40.530
And we are-- we are, in
our supervision with banks,

39:40.530 --> 39:44.990
as I indicated, we have
issued supervisory guidance

39:44.990 --> 39:48.640
to make sure that
underwriting standards are sound

39:48.640 --> 39:52.580
on these loans, and
we're aware--

39:52.580 --> 39:55.700
this is something also that
we look at in stress tests

39:55.700 --> 40:00.160
of the large-- the larger
banks to see what would happen

40:00.160 --> 40:02.900
to their capital
positions and to make sure

40:02.900 --> 40:05.080
that they hold sufficient
capital.

40:05.080 --> 40:09.660
And, of course, I think
the soundness and state

40:09.660 --> 40:13.200
of the banking system has
improved substantially, but,

40:13.200 --> 40:17.030
of course, we are
focused on such things.

40:17.030 --> 40:18.610
PETER BARNES.

40:18.610 --> 40:20.200
Chair Yellen, over here.

40:20.200 --> 40:24.440
Hi. Quick question on regulation
and the scandal at Wells Fargo

40:24.440 --> 40:26.050
over phony customer accounts.

40:26.050 --> 40:30.220
I know that there are other
regulators that have looked

40:30.220 --> 40:34.250
into this, but you are also
a regulator of Wells Fargo.

40:34.250 --> 40:37.320
Have-- has the Fed opened
a separate investigation

40:37.320 --> 40:41.450
into this-- these
practices at Wells Fargo?

40:41.450 --> 40:45.030
And-- and wouldn't you,
because they do involve issues

40:45.030 --> 40:48.820
of consumer protection,
potentially of risk management

40:48.820 --> 40:54.090
and corporate governance, and
are you looking at them broadly

40:54.090 --> 40:56.870
across the banking
system right now?

40:56.870 --> 40:57.630
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:57.630 --> 41:01.510
So, in this specific case,

41:01.510 --> 41:06.160
the abuses that occurred took
place in a national bank.

41:06.160 --> 41:10.070
The Comptroller of the Currency
has responsibility there.

41:10.070 --> 41:14.680
And on the consumer
side, it is the CFPB

41:14.680 --> 41:20.010
that has responsibility, but we
work cooperatively and closely

41:20.010 --> 41:22.070
with those organizations.

41:22.070 --> 41:28.000
And in terms of our overall
supervisory responsibility

41:28.000 --> 41:32.100
for Wells and other large
banking organizations,

41:32.100 --> 41:38.090
we are very focused, and this
will be a particular focus

41:38.090 --> 41:44.840
of our supervision going
forward over the next year or so

41:44.840 --> 41:50.430
on the compliance environment
to make sure that the controls,

41:50.430 --> 41:54.360
that the senior management
oversight, that the involvement

41:54.360 --> 41:57.650
of the boards of
directors are appropriate

41:57.650 --> 42:00.810
to control these kinds of risks.

42:00.810 --> 42:06.850
We have been distressed to see
banking organizations responding

42:06.850 --> 42:10.520
when a particular
problem arises,

42:10.520 --> 42:17.080
and what we'd really want to see
is robust procedures that ensure

42:17.080 --> 42:22.460
that employees are always acting
in a legal and ethical manner,

42:22.460 --> 42:24.670
and that the incentives
that are put in place

42:24.670 --> 42:27.820
in these organizations are
appropriate and don't serve

42:27.820 --> 42:31.840
to foster behavior that
could harm the public.

42:31.840 --> 42:36.380
And this has been and will be
a focus of our supervision.

42:36.380 --> 42:38.420
JIM TANKERSLEY.

42:38.420 --> 42:42.880
Hi. Jim Tankersley,
Washington Post.

42:42.880 --> 42:45.700
Over the past year we've seen
American policymakers begin

42:45.700 --> 42:49.180
to have maybe our most serious
discussion about tariffs

42:49.180 --> 42:51.520
in the last several decades.

42:51.520 --> 42:55.590
If tariffs were to be enacted
in the coming year or so,

42:55.590 --> 42:57.470
does the Fed have
an opinion on what

42:57.470 --> 42:59.260
that would do to
growth in America?

42:59.260 --> 43:00.060
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:00.060 --> 43:04.600
So, you know, that's a political
issue that's currently being

43:04.600 --> 43:08.160
debated that I really
don't want to get into.

43:08.160 --> 43:14.000
You know, so-- I'm going to--
I'm going to pass on that one.

43:14.000 --> 43:16.790
JOHN HELTMAN.

43:16.790 --> 43:18.820
Hi. John Heltman
with American Banker.

43:18.820 --> 43:22.260
Back to a question about
Wells or related to Wells.

43:22.260 --> 43:24.390
One of the concerns
that has been raised--

43:24.390 --> 43:26.130
that this scandal has raised--

43:26.130 --> 43:29.900
is that the bank itself says it
doesn't know what was happening,

43:29.900 --> 43:32.080
and there are thousands
of employees

43:32.080 --> 43:33.110
that were involved in this.

43:33.110 --> 43:35.940
Some are calling for a breakup,

43:35.940 --> 43:38.570
saying that the banks
are too big to manage.

43:38.570 --> 43:41.410
Do you think that--
leaving aside the question

43:41.410 --> 43:44.020
of Wells specifically, do
you think that it's possible

43:44.020 --> 43:47.000
for a bank to get so big
that it can't be managed,

43:47.000 --> 43:50.010
and that perhaps the best
prudential step would be

43:50.010 --> 43:52.530
to break it up?

43:52.530 --> 43:53.940
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:53.940 --> 44:01.620
So we have high expectations for
what we expect to be in place

44:01.620 --> 44:06.650
in a large organization or
in any banking organization.

44:06.650 --> 44:12.770
We expect there to be robust
systems of risk management,

44:12.770 --> 44:18.890
strong audit functions, a board
of directors that is monitoring

44:18.890 --> 44:25.480
and supervising and holding
senior management accountable

44:25.480 --> 44:28.510
for things that happen
throughout the organization

44:28.510 --> 44:32.450
in a strong compliance
environment, and I don't think

44:32.450 --> 44:35.440
that these are impossible
standards to meet.

44:35.440 --> 44:39.260
They may be challenging, but I
wouldn't at this point arrive

44:39.260 --> 44:41.120
at the conclusion that just

44:41.120 --> 44:44.210
because an organization
is large, it can't live

44:44.210 --> 44:45.570
up to those standards.

44:45.570 --> 44:48.580
And those are our
expectations, and we intend

44:48.580 --> 44:55.220
to hold banking organizations
responsible for putting in place

44:55.220 --> 44:58.150
that kind of-- that
kind of risk-management

44:58.150 --> 45:00.890
and compliance environment.

45:00.890 --> 45:03.430
JOHN HELTMAN.

45:03.430 --> 45:04.440
So, "No"? CHAIR YELLEN.

45:04.440 --> 45:08.120
So I'm not-- I'm not
endorsing a general conclusion

45:08.120 --> 45:12.000
that banks are-- banks of that
size are too big to manage.

45:12.000 --> 45:14.940
I believe they can be,
but it may be challenging,

45:14.940 --> 45:17.030
and that's what we expect.

45:17.030 --> 45:19.520
ERIK SCHATZKER.

45:21.100 --> 45:22.650
Erik Schatzker from
Bloomberg Television.

45:22.650 --> 45:23.840
Madam Chair, thank you.

45:23.840 --> 45:26.800
I have a question about the
rate trajectory the Fed outlined

45:26.800 --> 45:28.620
today in the dot plot.

45:28.620 --> 45:32.530
While there is clearly a wide
range, the median expectation is

45:32.530 --> 45:36.750
for the fed funds target to rise
by 1/2 percentage point in 2017,

45:36.750 --> 45:41.490
3/4 of a point in 2018, and a
further 3/4 of a point in 2019,

45:41.490 --> 45:46.140
bringing us to 2-1/2 to 2-3/4
percent, and then 2-3/4 percent

45:46.140 --> 45:48.420
to 3 percent in the long run.

45:48.420 --> 45:50.580
At the same time,
the median forecast

45:50.580 --> 45:53.770
for GDP growth is 2 percent
for the next two years

45:53.770 --> 45:56.680
and 1.8 percent thereafter.

45:56.680 --> 45:59.040
And, I should add, the most
optimistic projection is

45:59.040 --> 46:00.780
for growth of just 2-1/2 percent

46:00.780 --> 46:04.100
of all the projections
outlined here.

46:04.100 --> 46:09.560
So if economic growth is going
to be that slow for that long,

46:09.560 --> 46:11.930
where will the inflationary
forces emerge

46:11.930 --> 46:16.450
that would require
tightening of 250 basis points

46:16.450 --> 46:18.330
from where we are now?

46:18.330 --> 46:23.560
And if not inflation, is
there some other explanation?

46:23.560 --> 46:24.690
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:24.690 --> 46:27.410
So the projections-- I agree,

46:27.410 --> 46:29.640
the projections for
growth are slow.

46:29.640 --> 46:34.140
We have further written
down our estimate

46:34.140 --> 46:38.110
of the longer-run
normal growth rate.

46:38.110 --> 46:41.110
And what that reflects
is an assessment

46:41.110 --> 46:44.630
that productivity growth
is likely to remain low

46:44.630 --> 46:49.620
for an extended time, although
it does embody an expectation

46:49.620 --> 46:53.970
that it will pick up from the
miserable 1/2 percent pace per

46:53.970 --> 46:59.110
year that we have seen
over the last five years.

46:59.110 --> 47:05.400
Now, why we would never--
and slow growth is a factor.

47:05.400 --> 47:08.630
Slow productivity
growth is a factor

47:08.630 --> 47:14.510
that influences the longer-run
normal level of interest rates,

47:14.510 --> 47:17.530
and writing down the likely pace

47:17.530 --> 47:22.510
of productivity growth is one
factor that is responsible

47:22.510 --> 47:25.960
for the downward shift
in the path that you see

47:25.960 --> 47:27.930
for the federal funds rate.

47:27.930 --> 47:33.310
That's an important reason for
revising down the neutral rate.

47:33.310 --> 47:35.000
But now, let's go to your--

47:35.000 --> 47:37.580
the part of your
question about inflation.

47:37.580 --> 47:41.860
In spite of having
such slow growth,

47:41.860 --> 47:46.090
disappointing productivity
growth, we have a labor market

47:46.090 --> 47:48.960
that last year generated
an average

47:48.960 --> 47:52.550
of about 230,000 jobs a month

47:52.550 --> 47:55.730
and so far this year
has been generating

47:55.730 --> 47:58.880
about 180,000 jobs a month.

47:58.880 --> 48:04.250
And that is a very solid
pace of job growth and a pace

48:04.250 --> 48:08.100
that likely is not
sustainable in the longer run,

48:08.100 --> 48:09.670
although we've been pleased

48:09.670 --> 48:12.420
to see people come back
in the labor market.

48:12.420 --> 48:17.580
So it certainly is sustainable
for some further amount of time.

48:17.580 --> 48:21.500
But I think what ultimately
drives inflation, both wage

48:21.500 --> 48:25.210
and price growth, is that
tightness in the labor market

48:25.210 --> 48:28.240
and pressure on resource
utilization.

48:28.240 --> 48:33.820
And the sad fact is that we
are getting that healthy pace

48:33.820 --> 48:39.220
of job market growth with
very slow growth in output.

48:39.220 --> 48:44.070
So this is-- I don't think it
bears on the inflation outlook.

48:44.070 --> 48:50.120
It has prompted a downward
shift in the projected path

48:50.120 --> 48:53.630
for the neutral and
actual federal funds rate,

48:53.630 --> 48:57.760
but it is a huge concern
because slow productivity growth

48:57.760 --> 49:01.300
ultimately means slow
growth in living standards.

49:01.300 --> 49:02.660
And that's a big concern

49:02.660 --> 49:05.560
that policymakers
should be focused on.

49:05.560 --> 49:08.290
VICTORIA GUIDA.

49:08.290 --> 49:10.910
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

49:10.910 --> 49:11.980
Back to Wells Fargo.

49:11.980 --> 49:16.360
You know, obviously, this was
more of a consumer finance kind

49:16.360 --> 49:19.500
of a question, but I'm wondering
if you think it does pose safety

49:19.500 --> 49:20.900
and soundness questions,
if something

49:20.900 --> 49:23.460
like this is widespread
across a big bank.

49:23.460 --> 49:25.110
And you had mentioned
that this is going

49:25.110 --> 49:27.970
to be a supervisory focus
over the coming year.

49:27.970 --> 49:30.390
Are there any adjustments
that you can speak

49:30.390 --> 49:34.540
to that might be warranted,
given these revelations?

49:34.540 --> 49:35.660
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:35.660 --> 49:38.410
So, as I mentioned, we
are going to be focusing

49:38.410 --> 49:44.150
on compliance risk management
and board oversight not only

49:44.150 --> 49:49.330
at Wells, but also across
bank holding companies.

49:49.330 --> 49:54.130
Of course, consumer issues
and issues that involve harm

49:54.130 --> 49:58.060
of consumers can become
safety and soundness issues.

49:58.060 --> 50:02.350
And if there was-- at
least one of the lessons

50:02.350 --> 50:06.850
from the financial crisis,
I think, is that abuses

50:06.850 --> 50:09.560
of consumers of the
sort that we see--

50:09.560 --> 50:13.990
saw in subprime lending
ultimately did become--

50:13.990 --> 50:17.470
become safety and
soundness issues.

50:17.470 --> 50:20.700
And so, of course, we
need to have that concern,

50:20.700 --> 50:22.340
and we'll focus there.

50:22.340 --> 50:25.840
I think-- I can't really at
this point give you specifics

50:25.840 --> 50:26.720
beyond that.

50:26.720 --> 50:30.090
MICHAEL DERBY.

50:30.090 --> 50:32.670
Hi, Mike Derby from
Dow Jones Newswires.

50:32.670 --> 50:35.080
A large number of
congressional Democrats as well

50:35.080 --> 50:37.150
as the campaign of
Hillary Clinton would

50:37.150 --> 50:40.370
like bankers removed from the
boards overseeing the regional

50:40.370 --> 50:41.670
Fed banks.

50:41.670 --> 50:45.740
Also, other reformers would like
to see the private ownership

50:45.740 --> 50:47.850
of the regional--
the bank ownership

50:47.850 --> 50:49.120
of the regional Feds ended

50:49.120 --> 50:52.620
and that the regional Feds be
brought fully into government,

50:52.620 --> 50:56.720
and I wanted to know what you
thought of those two proposals.

50:56.720 --> 50:57.880
CHAIR YELLEN.

50:57.880 --> 51:00.710
So we have a system
that Congress did set

51:00.710 --> 51:04.090
up in the Federal Reserve
Act in which the governance

51:04.090 --> 51:10.690
of the Reserve Banks involves
banks contributing capital

51:10.690 --> 51:15.060
and serving on the
boards of directors.

51:15.060 --> 51:19.280
We have long recognized
inside the Federal Reserve

51:19.280 --> 51:24.350
that when we're charged
with supervision of banks,

51:24.350 --> 51:28.540
having bankers involved in that
obviously presents conflict

51:28.540 --> 51:29.490
of interest.

51:29.490 --> 51:35.320
And we have put in place very
strong measures to ensure

51:35.320 --> 51:38.750
that those conflicts of
interest are not allowed to play

51:38.750 --> 51:42.560
out in any-- in any way,
that bankers are not allowed

51:42.560 --> 51:45.010
to be involved in supervision.

51:45.010 --> 51:47.670
Dodd-Frank changed
the arrangement

51:47.670 --> 51:51.880
so that only the Class
B and C, or nonbanking,

51:51.880 --> 51:55.280
directors can participate
in the selection

51:55.280 --> 51:57.180
of the president as well.

51:57.180 --> 52:03.230
So I think-- I want to make sure
the public has confidence that,

52:03.230 --> 52:08.080
in spite of the fact that we
do have this banker involvement

52:08.080 --> 52:12.430
in our boards of directors,
that it is not giving rise

52:12.430 --> 52:17.690
to any conflicts in our
actual conduct of policy.

52:17.690 --> 52:27.220
Now, that setup-- if that setup
is changed, it raises which--

52:27.220 --> 52:30.560
it's up to Congress to
decide what to do here.

52:30.560 --> 52:33.930
It raises complex issues
about the governance--

52:33.930 --> 52:37.020
the whole governance
arrangement in the Reserve Banks

52:37.020 --> 52:40.090
in the Federal Reserve, and I
would simply caution that if

52:40.090 --> 52:44.520
that is looked at, as
Congress is entitled to do,

52:44.520 --> 52:48.340
that they think through
carefully what the ramifications

52:48.340 --> 52:51.950
of making changes would be.

52:51.950 --> 52:55.550
KAREN MRACEK.

52:55.550 --> 53:02.060
Karen Mracek with Market
News International.

53:02.060 --> 53:03.810
You mentioned in a
previous answer the need

53:03.810 --> 53:06.980
to be forward looking,
but you've also pointed

53:06.980 --> 53:10.480
to the economy not overheating
as a reason you could, you know,

53:10.480 --> 53:12.740
hold off on raising
rates at this one.

53:12.740 --> 53:15.090
Monetary policy has
traditionally operated

53:15.090 --> 53:17.370
with long and variable lags.

53:17.370 --> 53:19.880
Do you think this
timeline has changed

53:19.880 --> 53:23.270
since the financial
crisis or due to the use

53:23.270 --> 53:26.800
of unconventional tools that
the Fed used, and how does

53:26.800 --> 53:28.220
that factor into
your decisionmaking?

53:28.220 --> 53:29.360
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:29.360 --> 53:33.880
So I think the notion that
monetary policy operates

53:33.880 --> 53:36.730
with long and variable lags--

53:36.730 --> 53:40.820
that statement is due to
Milton Friedman, and it is one

53:40.820 --> 53:43.160
of the essential
things to understand

53:43.160 --> 53:44.920
about monetary policy,

53:44.920 --> 53:48.380
and it has not fundamentally
changed at all.

53:48.380 --> 53:53.280
And that is why I believe we
have to be forward looking,

53:53.280 --> 53:57.380
and I'm not in favor
of a "whites

53:57.380 --> 53:59.510
of their eyes" sort of approach.

53:59.510 --> 54:02.760
We need to operate
based on forecasts.

54:02.760 --> 54:08.400
But the global economy and the
U.S. economy have changed a lot.

54:08.400 --> 54:12.290
History doesn't always
exactly replay itself.

54:12.290 --> 54:16.950
Many of the-- those of us
sitting around the table,

54:16.950 --> 54:22.240
we learned the lesson that if
policy is not forward looking,

54:22.240 --> 54:27.220
that inflation can pick up
to highly undesirable levels,

54:27.220 --> 54:31.570
that inflation expectations
can be dislodged upward,

54:31.570 --> 54:36.590
and the consequence of that
can be that, endemically,

54:36.590 --> 54:39.140
higher inflation
takes place, which--

54:39.140 --> 54:42.700
it is very costly to reduce.

54:42.700 --> 54:48.780
And absolutely none of us want
to relive an episode like that.

54:48.780 --> 54:53.930
And so I believe, and my
colleagues, that it is important

54:53.930 --> 54:55.190
to be forward looking.

54:55.190 --> 54:58.430
We're not going to make
that mistake again.

54:58.430 --> 55:01.880
But the structure of the economy
changes, things do change.

55:01.880 --> 55:06.230
The nature of the inflation
process has changed, I think,

55:06.230 --> 55:10.610
significantly since the bad days
of the '70s when the Fed had

55:10.610 --> 55:14.970
to face this chronic
high-inflation problem.

55:14.970 --> 55:19.770
We've seen inflation
respond less to the economy,

55:19.770 --> 55:23.180
to movements in the unemployment
rate-- that's sometimes said,

55:23.180 --> 55:25.460
"The Phillips curve
has become flatter."

55:25.460 --> 55:27.800
So we've seen less
of a response.

55:27.800 --> 55:32.250
That's something we need to
factor into our decisionmaking.

55:32.250 --> 55:36.530
Inflation expectations
appear to be better anchored,

55:36.530 --> 55:40.370
and perhaps that's been
a result of a long period

55:40.370 --> 55:42.690
of low and stable inflation.

55:42.690 --> 55:43.510
That's an asset.

55:43.510 --> 55:47.630
It's something we
didn't have in the 1970s.

55:47.630 --> 55:49.610
And, in addition, we
have to be attentive

55:49.610 --> 55:52.100
to the fact there we've
now had a long period

55:52.100 --> 55:55.210
in which inflation is
actually undershooting our 2

55:55.210 --> 55:56.660
percent objective.

55:56.660 --> 55:59.020
And we see some signs that I--

55:59.020 --> 56:03.420
we conclude inflation
expectations are reasonably well

56:03.420 --> 56:05.290
anchored at 2 percent.

56:05.290 --> 56:08.510
But we are seeing signs
suggesting possible slippage

56:08.510 --> 56:11.270
there, and we're a
long way from being--

56:11.270 --> 56:14.150
facing the problems
that Japan faces.

56:14.150 --> 56:15.930
But there always a remi--

56:15.930 --> 56:19.960
should be a reminder to us
that we also would not want

56:19.960 --> 56:21.880
to find ourselves in a period

56:21.880 --> 56:24.960
where inflation is
chronically low--

56:24.960 --> 56:26.740
running below our objective,

56:26.740 --> 56:29.640
inflation expectations
are slipping.

56:29.640 --> 56:32.460
And with a low neutral rate,
that becomes more important.

56:32.460 --> 56:34.990
So things are changed,
but the principle

56:34.990 --> 56:37.880
of "forward looking"
absolutely holds.

56:37.880 --> 56:40.200
PATRICK GILLESPIE.

56:40.200 --> 56:41.930
Hi, Patrick Gillespie with CNN.

56:41.930 --> 56:43.210
Chair Yellen, you
just mentioned one

56:43.210 --> 56:46.030
of the economy's major problems:
low productivity growth.

56:46.030 --> 56:49.480
And one of the solutions that's
been proposed widely is better

56:49.480 --> 56:50.960
job skills.

56:50.960 --> 56:53.780
Many economists say that
some workers are staying

56:53.780 --> 56:55.260
on the sidelines
because they don't--

56:55.260 --> 56:56.870
they, you know, they
lack new job skills,

56:56.870 --> 56:59.830
the ones that would help them
obtain better employment.

56:59.830 --> 57:02.280
The Fed doesn't have
the authority to finance

57:02.280 --> 57:05.620
or run its own job training
program or apprenticeship,

57:05.620 --> 57:08.030
but would you want that
authority from Congress?

57:08.030 --> 57:11.360
And is it at all
frustrating that you know--

57:11.360 --> 57:13.280
you and your colleagues
know one of the solutions

57:13.280 --> 57:15.430
to fixing a major
issue in the economy,

57:15.430 --> 57:18.930
but you can't take the concrete
steps to solving that issue?

57:18.930 --> 57:19.560
CHAIR YELLEN.

57:19.560 --> 57:26.370
Well, I do think job training
and job skills are important.

57:26.370 --> 57:31.750
And we do work in community
development, trying to--

57:31.750 --> 57:36.000
in the local communities where
the Reserve Banks operate,

57:36.000 --> 57:41.360
to try to foster broader
understanding of what kinds

57:41.360 --> 57:47.640
of programs work and how
community organizations in state

57:47.640 --> 57:49.180
and local governments can put

57:49.180 --> 57:52.060
in place programs
that will be helpful.

57:52.060 --> 57:55.640
I've recently visited a program

57:55.640 --> 57:57.840
that was very impressive
in Philadelphia.

57:57.840 --> 58:00.790
I've visited some in
Chicago and other places.

58:00.790 --> 58:05.430
So I do believe it's
possible to design programs

58:05.430 --> 58:08.980
that will help people
overcome obstacles

58:08.980 --> 58:11.680
in getting jobs that
are available.

58:11.680 --> 58:13.380
But I definitely
think that, you know,

58:13.380 --> 58:18.120
while we can play some role
in facilitating understanding

58:18.120 --> 58:21.370
of what works and what doesn't
work that can be helpful,

58:21.370 --> 58:24.640
it's certainly worthwhile
for policymakers

58:24.640 --> 58:29.390
at the federal level, at state
and local levels, to be focusing

58:29.390 --> 58:31.950
on this, because I
think it is an area

58:31.950 --> 58:35.320
that would be helpful
in making progress.

58:35.320 --> 58:38.290
MARK HAMRICK.

58:38.290 --> 58:41.310
Thank you, Madam Chair, Mark
Hamrick with Bankrate.com.

58:41.310 --> 58:44.900
As we've gotten to this 4.9
percent unemployment rate,

58:44.900 --> 58:47.310
as you know, through this
year's long recovery, we have

58:47.310 --> 58:50.530
yet to see substantial
pickup in wage growth.

58:50.530 --> 58:53.200
And it seems as if the
American middle class continues

58:53.200 --> 58:55.720
to express some disappointment
about that.

58:55.720 --> 58:58.030
Is there better news
on the horizon?

58:58.030 --> 59:00.550
Do you think it could
come in 2017,

59:00.550 --> 59:02.630
for example, and if not, when?

59:02.630 --> 59:03.360
CHAIR YELLEN.

59:03.360 --> 59:09.300
So I think we have seen some
modest pickup in wage growth.

59:09.300 --> 59:11.930
It's running a little
bit higher than it was

59:11.930 --> 59:17.480
over the last two years by a
number of important measures.

59:17.480 --> 59:21.900
And we have seen income
growth pick up recently.

59:21.900 --> 59:26.530
I think the Census
report was encouraging,

59:26.530 --> 59:31.760
showing that there are income
gains, both because of more jobs

59:31.760 --> 59:35.960
and higher-paying jobs, and that
that's occurring throughout the

59:35.960 --> 59:37.310
income distribution.

59:37.310 --> 59:39.090
It's helping many families.

59:39.090 --> 59:44.620
But I do expect-- we
expect the unemployment rate

59:44.620 --> 59:48.070
to decline further, we expect
labor market conditions

59:48.070 --> 59:50.460
to continue to improve.

59:50.460 --> 59:52.550
And my hope and expectation is

59:52.550 --> 59:55.670
that we will see some
further pickup in wage growth,

59:55.670 --> 59:58.690
and that it will be
broadly beneficial

59:58.690 --> 01:00:00.070
to American households.

